From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:08:43 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] base: a message window that cannot be made is not memory
 running out

On Windows 10 Mobile RegisterClassEx is a stub, so registering the
message-only window class always fails, and MessageWindow treated that as
resource exhaustion and ended the process. Chrome's browser layer reaches
it a few seconds after start (WebUsbDetector -> DeviceMonitorWin), and the
content layer on demand (WebHID, WebAuthn's HID discovery, Web Serial).
Now the class is left unregistered and MessageWindow::Create fails, which
its users already handle.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 base/win/message_window.cc | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/base/win/message_window.cc b/base/win/message_window.cc
index 01fb5d9b5a2f72c5ac66354b38e1424ddadf36d7..92f85dd0ca350f12aa94cc82920e9fb903706225 100644
--- a/base/win/message_window.cc
+++ b/base/win/message_window.cc
@@ -123,7 +123,15 @@ MessageWindow::WindowClass::WindowClass() {
   if (atom_ == 0) {
     PLOG(ERROR)
         << "Failed to register the window class for a message-only window";
+#if defined(VENETIUM_W10M)
+    // On Windows 10 Mobile an app has no window classes (RegisterClassEx is
+    // a stub there), which says nothing about memory: every MessageWindow
+    // fails to be created instead, and its users go on without one -- the
+    // device monitor behind WebUSB, WebHID and WebAuthn, the gamepad's raw
+    // input.
+#else
     OnResourceExhausted();
+#endif
   }
 }
 
@@ -173,6 +181,13 @@ bool MessageWindow::DoCreate(MessageCallback message_callback,
   message_callback_ = std::move(message_callback);
 
   WindowClass& window_class = g_window_class.Get();
+#if defined(VENETIUM_W10M)
+  // Before CreateWindow, whose stub leaves the last error as it was: one
+  // left at ERROR_NOT_ENOUGH_MEMORY would end the process below.
+  if (!window_class.atom()) {
+    return false;
+  }
+#endif
   window_ =
       CreateWindow(MAKEINTATOM(window_class.atom()), window_name, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0,
                    HWND_MESSAGE, nullptr, window_class.instance(), this);
